ERAS = [
 {"id":"e1","from":1600,"to":1729.6,"span":"1671 to 1729","ch":"Ch. I","name":"A kingdom with no centre",
  "text":"Travancore in 1671 was less a kingdom than a family quarrel. Three branches (swarupam) of one royal kin group, the Trippappur house of Venad, the Jayasimhanad house of Quilon and the Cheraway house of Kayamkulam, competed for the senior title of kulasekhara perumal, which carried a half seat on the council of the Padmanabhaswami temple at Trivandrum. Real power lay with the pillamar, temple administrators who had become territorial lords with their own nayar militias, and with the madumbimar of the smaller states. When Aditiya Varma died in 1677 the pillamar closed the temple, called in the Nedumangadu prince and drove the regent Asvati Tirunal to Varkalai. For fifty years the throne was kept alive by adoptions from Kolathiri in the far north (1681, 1696, 1718). Each adopted ruler who tried to pay for an army out of trade was opposed, and the prince of Kottayam was murdered in 1696. Madurai raided Nanjanad for tribute, the Dutch and English traded where they dared, and in 1721 the Attingal crowd massacred the English party on its way to the rani.",
  "why":"The book's argument starts here: the kingdom fell apart because kingship had become a prize the nobles could auction."},
 {"id":"e2","from":1729.6,"to":1731,"span":"1729 to 1730","ch":"Ch. II","name":"The new king breaks the nobles",
  "text":"Martanda Varma came to the throne in 1729, aged 24, the son of a Kolathiri princess and a Kilimanur prince, brought up in Karunagappalli. His cousins Pappu Tambi and Rama Tambi claimed the throne with the pillamar and Madurai cavalry. De Lannoy reads a 1742 letter of the king to show they were sons of his own elder brother, the prince of Neyyatinkara, and not of the late king as the Travancore histories say. The king lost Kottar and fled to Quilon in May 1730, but the unpaid Madurai troops ran wild and the pillamar themselves recalled him. In October the Tambis were killed in the palace at Kalkulam. In November a plot to kill him at the Panguni Uttaram festival was betrayed by an innkeeper and crushed. Their estates went to the crown and were worked by their former tenants, many of them not nayar.",
  "why":"From now on the king paid his own officials and soldiers instead of renting power from the nobility."},
 {"id":"e3","from":1731,"to":1739,"span":"1731 to 1738","ch":"Ch. II and V","name":"Wars with the cousins",
  "text":"With Venad in hand, Martanda Varma turned on the collateral princes: Desinganadu at Quilon, the Kayamkulam prince, and the two vacant thrones both sides claimed by adoption, Nedumangadu and Karunagappalli. The wars of 1732 to 1735 swung back and forth, with Madurai raids from the south and Cochin money behind Kayamkulam. In June 1734 the Kayamkulam prince was killed in his own lines and chaver were sent to avenge him. In April 1736 Nedumangadu, the Dutch 'pepper-storeroom of Malabar', was annexed and its pepper sent overland to the Coromandel coast, past the Dutch. To pay for the army Thanu Pillai brought in the first land tax (1736) and customs were pushed up; Ramayyan, a Tamil Brahmin from Tirunelveli district, became dalawa in 1737. By 1738 Desinganadu, Cochin and Tekkumkur had allied with open Dutch backing, and the first Aramboli Lines went up against Madurai.",
  "why":"Every gain cost money the old revenues could not supply, which pushed the king toward land tax, monopolies and foreign soldiers."},
 {"id":"e4","from":1739,"to":1741.13,"span":"1739 to February 1741","ch":"Ch. III","name":"The Dutch choose war",
  "text":"Gustaaf Willem van Imhoff, governor of Ceylon, came to Malabar in 1739 to restore the balance of power. He met the king at midnight on the beach at Tengapattanam in February, got nothing, and advised Batavia to take Nedumangadu and Karunagappalli for the Company's allies. In September the VOC allied with Desinganadu, Cochin, Purakkad and Tekkumkur. Hackert's companies broke the Travancore lines south of Quilon; the allies marched down the coast over Varkalai hill and failed before the Attingal palace in February 1740, while English Anjengo armed the rani. In the south the Nawab of Arcot's army plundered Suchindram, and in the north the Nedumangadu princess-regent was restored. The king signed Colachel over to the king of France. Van Imhoff's answer was a sea-borne plan: blockade every port, bombard Colachel (November 1740) and land a force to choke the trade of Kottar. De Lannoy argues this misread a land power whose pepper and cloth could still go overland.",
  "why":"A trading company decided to fight a land war, and chose to fight it from the beach."},
 {"id":"e5","from":1741.13,"to":1741.63,"span":"February to August 1741","ch":"Ch. III and V","name":"Colachel",
  "text":"The Dutch landed at Colachel on 19 February 1741 and began a European fort with mud bastions that collapsed, no lime and no water. Stein van Gollenesse left in April; Hackert sailed off to Tuticorin with the ships and most of the stores, leaving Rijtel with about 250 Europeans and 50 lascorins. From June the Travancoreans, directed by the deserter Duijvenschot with English guns and gunners, ran a real siege with batteries on the beach and some 20,000 men. The relief from Kanniyakumari turned back at the Aramboli Lines in July and was driven out of the Cobolon lines on 8 August. Rijtel was mortally wounded on 2 August, the day Eustache de Lannoy deserted. On 9 August a cannon ball fired the powder magazine in the church. De Lannoy talked the garrison into terms, signed on 12 August; on 13 August the 190 survivors marched out and were made prisoner.",
  "why":"It was less a battle than a starved fort, a failed relief and an explosion, but it broke the coalition."},
 {"id":"e6","from":1741.63,"to":1744,"span":"August 1741 to 1743","ch":"Ch. III and V","name":"The coalition collapses",
  "text":"Colachel broke the alliance. The Dutch left the south, Cochin pulled out and Karunagappalli was retaken. In the north Duijvenschot's European-drilled troops scattered Desinganadu's army at Kilimanur in April 1742 and took the Dutch forts Hollandia and Seeburg without a fight, and Kayamkulam was laid waste. The siege of Quilon failed in July 1742, but Desinganadu made peace at Manaddi in September and became a tributary. The first treaty of Mavelikara (22 May 1743) promised the Dutch 1,200 candies of pepper a year; Batavia refused to ratify it. To deliver that pepper the state made it a monopoly, run by Ramayyan from Mavelikara with a fixed budget, and the land tax became permanent. De Lannoy retrained the palace guard at the end of 1742.",
  "why":"The Dutch defeat, in de Lannoy's phrase, laid the foundation of the pepper monopoly."},
 {"id":"e7","from":1744,"to":1753,"span":"1744 to 1752","ch":"Ch. IV and V","name":"The new army goes north",
  "text":"With De Lannoy commanding the palace guard and rebuilding Udayagiri, the reformed army moved north. The Nawab of Trichinopoly was paid off in 1745 with money squeezed from Tekkumkur. Desinganadu, now allied with Tekkumkur, lost Quilon for good when De Lannoy's men took its lines on 10 October 1747: 'the last remnant of ancien régime Travancore disappeared'. Tekkumkur, Vadakkumkur and Purakkad were set against each other, invaded and made subject by November 1750, and Tekkumkur's people were put to building defence lines. In January 1750 the king gave his kingdom to Sri Padmanabha and ruled as the god's servant. A Cochin succession quarrel gave him a protégé, the Chazhur Tamban, inside Cochin. In 1752 the Brahmin prince of Purakkad lost his state.",
  "why":"The conquests that made modern Travancore came after 1743, with the new army."},
 {"id":"e8","from":1753,"to":1758.6,"span":"1753 to 1758","ch":"Ch. IV","name":"The second Mavelikara treaty and the last wars",
  "text":"The second treaty of Mavelikara (15 August 1753) bought Dutch neutrality and, in effect, Dutch acceptance of future conquests. The northern princes rose at once, took Mavelikara and nearly undid ten years of expansion; Madurai politics, the monsoon and the king's recovery saved him. Bribery opened the Tottapolli lines in 1754, an earth wall more than 33 km long was raised from Vaikom to the Ghats, and in 1755 Travancore burnt Thrippunithura, the mother-house of the Cochin dynasty, while De Lannoy threw back the Nawab's army in the south. Ramayyan died in January 1756 and Todupuzha fell. In 1757 the Cochin king stood silent before Martanda Varma at Thrippunithura. The king died in 1758, having united the coast from Kanniyakumari to Cochin.",
  "why":"By 1758 the Dutch were bystanders and Cochin was a client."},
 {"id":"e9","from":1758.6,"to":1900,"span":"1758 to 1809","ch":"Ch. V","name":"Afterwards",
  "text":"Under Rama Varma, the Dharma Raja, Poku Mussa was trampled to death (1758), the Travancore Lines were begun north of Cochin in 1761, and De Lannoy served until his death at Udayagiri in 1777. In 1790 the English alliance sent the Frenchmen away, and in 1809 Velu Tambi's revolt failed and with it Travancore's independence.",
  "why":"The book's epilogue: the state Martanda Varma built outlived him, but its factions only ended under the British."},
]

